<!--
  Daybook: a sun over an open almanac.

  Adaptive-icon foreground. The 108dp canvas has its outer 18dp masked away, and by a different
  amount on every launcher, so every mark here sits inside the 66dp circle that is visible under
  all of them: nothing strays further than 33 units from the centre at 54,54. That circle is why
  the book is a lens rather than a rectangle; a wide book with square bottom corners loses them
  under a circular mask.

  Four elements, because 48dp will not hold more: the moss board of the book, the two parchment
  pages sitting on it, the amber sun, and the sun's rays. Four things are deliberate.

  The gutter is a slot cut out of the board's own outline rather than a dark line drawn over it,
  so "open book" is carried by shape and survives into the themed-icon silhouette. It runs to
  within a few units of the bottom edge, leaving only a bridge of board below it, which is the
  spine. Shallower slots read as a notch in a bowl.

  The bottom of the board runs flat across that bridge rather than meeting in a point, because a
  point turns the flattened silhouette into an arrowhead.

  Each page is a parallelogram of even thickness rather than a wedge, with straight edges, tilted
  up towards the outside. A wedge tapering towards the spine reads as a boat hull.

  And the sun is rayed rather than a plain disc, because a bare disc centred above a wide curved
  shape reads as a head over a pair of shoulders. The rays are eight near-parallel bars set a clear
  gap out from the disc: wedges that flared outwards turned the sun into a flower, and thin
  tapered spikes vanished into specks at 48dp.

  ic_launcher_monochrome.xml is this same outline flattened; the two must stay in step.
-->
